<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Policarpo Toro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Policarpo Toro was a Chilean naval officer who annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a> (Rapa Nui) for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>. After visiting the island in 1870 and urging its acquisition, he bought land there from foreign owners and on 9 September 1888 signed an agreement of cession with the ariki <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atamu-tekena">Atamu Tekena</a> and other chiefs. Chile later leased most of the island to a company for sheep ranching.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/policarpo-toro.jpg" alt="Policarpo Toro" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Policarpo_Toro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Policarpo Toro Hurtado was born on 6 February 1856 in Melipilla, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, the son of farm owners. Drawn to the sea from an early age, he entered the State Naval School, now the Arturo Prat Naval School, as a cadet on 17 February 1871. In 1873 he took part in an expedition to the Strait of Magellan to draw up a plan of the city of Punta Arenas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Aboard the corvette O'Higgins he joined a scientific expedition to survey and map <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a>, where he was struck by the conditions of the islanders, who had suffered at the hands of expeditions seeking slaves and wealth. He then served for a time with the British Royal Navy, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> began in 1879. He took part in several actions, was promoted to first lieutenant and made second commander of the gunboat Magallanes.</p><p>In 1883 he became a professor at the Naval School. Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a> aboard the corvette Abtao, he found the islanders' lives worse than before and wrote a memoir to President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-manuel-balmaceda">José Manuel Balmaceda</a> setting out the advantages of acquiring the island. With the president's approval he negotiated with the French, with Tahiti and with the islanders under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atamu-tekena">Atamu Tekena</a>, so that no other nation would claim the island. The act of cession of 9 September 1888 was drawn up in Spanish and in Rapanui mixed with Tahitian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Toro was afterwards appointed director of the school for ship's boys. In the civil war of 1891 he stayed loyal to Balmaceda and refused to act against his government, and he was arrested and separated from the navy. In 1893, under the general amnesty, President Jorge Montt granted him a pension suited to his rank. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> in 1921 and is remembered as the man who brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Easter Island</a> into the Chilean state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Policarpo Toro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chile’s annexation in 1888 was carried out by a naval officer, Captain <b>Policarpo Toro</b>, without fighting.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rapa-nui">Rapa Nui · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Melipilla, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.69,-71.22&amp;year=1898" aria-label="Melipilla on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2732 3385 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Policarpo Toro"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3182 3655h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3182 3655h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.69,-71.22&amp;year=1898">What was here in 1898? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
